<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Hardy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Hardy was an English novelist, poet and architect, born in 1840 at Higher Bockhampton near Dorchester in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, the son of a stonemason. Apprenticed to a local architect at sixteen, he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1862 before turning to writing. His novels, among them Far from the Madding Crowd (1874), The Mayor of Casterbridge (1886), Tess of the d'Urbervilles (1891) and Jude the Obscure (1895), concern tragic characters struggling against their passions and social circumstances, most of them set in the semi-fictional region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. He regarded himself primarily as a poet, though his first collection appeared only in 1898, and younger poets viewed him as a mentor. He died in 1928.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-hardy.jpg" alt="Thomas Hardy" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Hardy_Restored_Portrait_1910–1915_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hardy's mother, Jemima Hand, was well read and taught him herself until he entered his first school at Bockhampton at the age of eight. He later attended Mr Last's Academy for Young Gentlemen in Dorchester, where he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. His family could not afford a university education, so his formal schooling ended at sixteen, when he was apprenticed to the local architect James Hicks.</p><p>In 1870, while restoring the parish church of St Juliot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, he met Emma Gifford, whom he married on 17 September 1874. In 1885 they moved to Max Gate in Dorchester, a house Hardy designed and his brother built. Emma's death in 1912 affected him deeply, and the Poems of 1912–13 reflect on it. In 1914 he married his secretary Florence Emily Dugdale, 39 years younger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hardy's first novel, The Poor Man and the Lady, was finished by 1867 but found no publisher. The poet and novelist George Meredith thought it too politically controversial and likely to harm his prospects. Hardy took the advice, destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> and reused some of its ideas later. He described it as socialistic, not to say revolutionary, though not argumentatively so.</p><p>His first volume of verse, Wessex Poems, appeared in 1898 and gathered poems written over thirty years. In the twentieth century he published only poetry, including Poems of the Past and the Present (1901), which contains The Darkling Thrush, and the three-part verse drama The Dynasts (1904–08). From 1908 he also worked with a local amateur group that became the Hardy Players, and wrote a one-act play for them in 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Hardy examined the social constraints on Victorian lives and criticised beliefs about marriage, education and religion that limited people and caused unhappiness. Philip Larkin saw suffering as the centre of his work. His family was Anglican but not especially devout, and he came to question the traditional Christian God, speaking instead of an unconscious will of the Universe.</p><p>From the 1880s he campaigned to save ancient buildings from destruction and became an early member of the Society for the Protection of Ancient Buildings. Younger poets, particularly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, regarded him as a mentor, and composers including Gerald Finzi, Benjamin Britten and Gustav Holst set his poems to music.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Hardy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
